                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "What is Dark Train?\n\nDark Train is an artistic steampunk puzzle adventure game by the Czech indie studio Paperash Studio. Everything is made out of paper and the whole art style is around hand-crafted papercut art. You play as the mechanical squid Ann 2.35f and need to control a train to deliver a mysterious order for deceased D. W. Tagrezbung, the famous inventor from the Border City. The developer promises that the game has no text, dialogue, tutorial or hint and this is what you get. This was fine in the beginning, but got frustrating for me so I dropped it somewhere around half the game.\n\nVisuals & Art Style\n\nLet's begin with the positive part of my review, the visuals. The papercut art style sounded promising and it delivered. The visuals are really nice to look at with its dark, atmospheric steampunk world. This is the main reason you could try the game, whether it's the world flying by the train or any of the separate rooms you travel to, everything has another good-looking scene.\n\nAudio\n\nSame with the audio, don't expect some crazy soundtrack, but the ambient sound design just felt fitting. It was never the main focus, but it did add to the atmosphere around the whole world.\n\nGameplay & Puzzles\n\nThe game will give you nothing, like described on their game pages, no hint, dialogue or tutorial, which I think is a very nice choice, a game explaining itself through game design, rather than random text boxes. However here it isn't even through the game design. The solution was sometimes obvious, but more commonly felt like pure accident to discover, I think there is a really small gap between challenging and directionless. Therefore at some point I started using a guide to get me through these less obvious parts, but that got me through puzzles still without an understanding on how I should've known and no satisfaction of solving it, which got me to drop it. The only part I can confidently say I understood myself was the water, fire, electricity part, however up until the point I played, it wasn't used all that much. The controls were fine. Ann's orientation follows your mouse movement, if you need her vertical but she's horizontal, there's no way to rotate her directly, so you have to move in a different direction first, which was sometimes frustrating.\n\nStory\n\nAgain nothing much to say, just like the game doesn't tell you all that much. I didn't get the narrative and all the information about transporting a mysterious order was from the Steam page and in no way really implied by the game. The rest of the parts I played also didn't feel like they followed a story I could talk about.\n\nWhy I dropped it\n\nIn the previous parts I talked partly about the why, but to get it all in one place, the game felt directionless. Sometimes not a single hint to where to go, which is why I used a guide on my second monitor later on. However this one I needed more and more as the game progresses and it felt like I was reading a playthrough, rather than playing a game. At the end a section that required a control I was never given, combined with a new ability by Ann I didn't know about and a maze with no visible structure got me to drop the game.\n\nFinal Thoughts\n\nIf you need any sense of direction or feedback, this game isn't for you in the slightest, the art style deserves to be seen, but the gameplay doesn't deserve to be endured. If, however, you love the pure experimentation to find out new things by trial and error, this might be something you can enjoy more than I did.",
